// Naive schedule for example 23-dot-product.
//
// Single worker `host`, every kernel placed there. Smoke test for the
// algorithm/schedule split end-to-end against this algorithm file, and
// the cross-backend differential workhorse: if `23-dot-product` parses,
// lowers, links, and emits reference-matching bytes under this schedule
// on every tier-1 backend, the map-reduce composition is sound.
//
// No `transfer` directives: with everything on one worker no data
// crosses worker boundaries. PRD §6.3.4 only requires `transfer` for
// cross-worker edges; the link pass enforces that.
//
// No loop transforms: no `block=`, no `vectorize=`, no `partition=`.
// The point of the smoke test is "the algorithm runs sequentially and
// produces the reference". A distributed schedule (partitioning the
// `w` axis, as sketched in 03-reduction) is out of scope here.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_input   on host;
    place load_input_b on host;
    place save_output  on host;
    place mul          on host;
    place accumulate   on host;
    place combine      on host;

    // No loop transforms.
    // No transfer directives — no cross-worker edges.
}
